{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact head fa430dc6db8fa0851725789f50b995d976558eb9 through artifact 0788df217b584e1818428c93d08f046299103bf0.\n\nThe six earlier corrections are substantively resolved: the inventory source is now one analytic fold and explicitly projects reservation rows; the result mapping matches the oracle's absent/present decision rows and Corpus C; Event now includes decoded payload and ordered rests_on; I8/I9 are repository-local deliveries with the Gitseq deletion and architecture update in one head; current Tailapps main is verified unchanged from v0.1.3 for the package and pins, and the DDL count is seven; only DialectComponent is mechanically content-hashed and every pin move remains corpus-gated. Nested-module, chess extend-first, workroom-native, and the detailed reconciliation remain sound.\n\nOne identity/input-contract blocker remains in correction 3. The note says host.canonicalization pins “the meta contract of section 2.7”, but section 2.7 never defines EvaluationInput.Meta. EvaluationInput includes Meta and Event, and the core validates neither. The note also says the six Event scalar “text conversions” are pinned but shows timestamp and payload_digest only as placeholders and does not state their exact conversion algorithms. Thus two implementations can supply different Meta maps or different timestamp/digest strings while both claim host.canonicalization=gitseq-record/1. That is the silent semantic drift this component is supposed to prevent.\n\nRepair narrowly: specify the exact Meta object, keys, types, and values supplied to the normalizer and analytic fold (or explicitly specify an empty object where unused); specify the exact conversion for each of the six Event scalars, including position, timestamp, and the bytes/encoding/prefix used for payload_digest; make the host.canonicalization description point to that actual normative contract; and require corpus coverage that freezes those bytes/values. Preserve the